(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-calcium-channel-blockers-quiz"]={"title":"Calcium Channel Blockers Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why are arterial vessels 3 to 10 times more sensitive to calcium channel blockers than myocardial cells?","choices":["Myocytes lack L-type calcium channels","Arteries store more internal calcium","Arteries carry more beta-2 receptors","Arteries rely on outside calcium entry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Arterial smooth muscle depends on exogenous calcium through L-type channels, whereas myocytes also draw on a recirculating store in the sarcoplasmic reticulum. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which pair of calcium channel blockers lowers heart rate and slows atrioventricular conduction?","choices":["Amlodipine and nifedipine","Felodipine and isradipine","Nicardipine and nimodipine","Diltiazem and verapamil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only the non-dihydropyridines strongly suppress the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes; this one distinction drives their indications, adverse effects and contraindications. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"At therapeutic doses, how do calcium channel blockers affect preload and afterload?","choices":["Lower afterload, no change in preload","Lower preload, no change in afterload","Lower preload and afterload equally","Raise preload and lower afterload"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At therapeutic doses they do not reduce venous tone, so they decrease afterload without affecting preload. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker is more likely than diltiazem to cause gynecomastia?","choices":["Verapamil","Nifedipine","Nimodipine","Nicardipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gynecomastia is listed with the comparison that nifedipine exceeds diltiazem; gingival hyperplasia is shared across the class. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient was admitted last week with unstable angina. Which calcium channel blocker formulation should be avoided?","choices":["Extended-release non-dihydropyridine","Immediate-release dihydropyridine","Intravenous non-dihydropyridine","Any oral non-dihydropyridine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unstable angina or recent myocardial infarction contraindicates immediate-release dihydropyridines, and short-acting formulations are to be avoided in general. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A transplant recipient taking tacrolimus is started on diltiazem. What is expected to happen to the tacrolimus level?","choices":["It falls","It is unchanged","It rises","It becomes unmeasurable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, and tacrolimus and cyclosporine are on both lists, so their levels rise. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What effect does adding a CYP3A4 inhibitor have on a non-dihydropyridine such as verapamil?","choices":["It lengthens its half-life","It shortens its half-life","It blocks its absorption","It has no effect on it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. CYP3A4 inhibitors increase the half-life of non-dihydropyridines, and of dihydropyridines as well. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient on simvastatin is started on verapamil and develops muscle aches. By what mechanism does verapamil raise simvastatin levels?","choices":["CYP3A4 induction","P-glycoprotein induction","Protein binding displacement","CYP3A4 inhibition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin, as well as carbamazepine, propranolol, tacrolimus and cyclosporine. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker?","choices":["Chronic stable angina","Essential hypertension","First-degree heart block","Severe aortic stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe aortic stenosis is a contraindication, as are unstable angina or recent myocardial infarction when an immediate-release formulation would be used. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient with hypertension also has heart failure. Which calcium channel blocker avoids the relative contraindication in heart failure?","choices":["Verapamil","Diltiazem","Amlodipine","Nimodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a relative contraindication to the non-dihydropyridines because they suppress contractility; amlodipine, a dihydropyridine approved for hypertension, has little effect on contractility. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which approved indication belongs to verapamil but not to amlodipine?","choices":["Supraventricular tachycardia","Chronic stable angina","Essential hypertension","Subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both treat angina and hypertension; only the non-dihydropyridines slow the atrioventricular node enough to treat supraventricular tachycardia. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How does verapamil raise digoxin levels?","choices":["CYP3A4 induction","Reduced renal blood flow","P-glycoprotein inhibition","Binding digoxin in the gut"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit P-glycoprotein, raising digoxin, tacrolimus, cyclosporine and carbamazepine; digoxin also adds a pharmacodynamic interaction. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which laboratory change is listed among the adverse effects of non-dihydropyridines?","choices":["Raised serum potassium","Raised liver function tests","Raised serum uric acid","Raised blood glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevations in liver function tests are listed, and liver disease is a relative contraindication to diltiazem and verapamil. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A patient started on nifedipine reports a pounding, rapid heartbeat. What explains it?","choices":["Direct stimulation of the sinus node","Blockade of cardiac beta-1 receptors","Reflex tachycardia from vasodilation","Hyperkalemia from renal effects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Strong arterial dilation drops resistance abruptly and the heart compensates by speeding up; rebound tachycardia is a listed dihydropyridine effect. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which is listed as a relative contraindication to non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers?","choices":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease","Asthma with frequent wheeze","Gout with raised uric acid","Hypokalemia on a diuretic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure, liver disease and gastroesophageal reflux disease are relative contraindications; the drugs relax smooth muscle, including the lower esophageal sphincter. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient on diltiazem is started on amiodarone. What pharmacodynamic risk does the combination carry?","choices":["Reflex tachycardia","Excess slowing of the heart","Hyperkalemia","Loss of diltiazem effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone with a non-dihydropyridine can slow the sinus rate or worsen atrioventricular block; digoxin and beta blockers are listed alongside it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
